the biggest question with scholar of the  first sin is who is it for because  they're not just porting the game to  next-gen they're actually adding  elements that might make you want to  play again they're incentivizing another  playthrough for old players and  enhancing the experience for new ones  and ultimately that is a good thing but  it's kind of forced you into this  awkward position where you have to ask  yourself the question is it worth it for  me to play through this game again and  everybody's going to have a different  answer for that some people might just  download the free patch On February 5th  and some people might repurchase the  entire game for their more powerful  systems in April both of these versions  will have different features so if you  want a list of those then you should  definitely check out the description  because it can get pretty confusing but  most of you are interested in the  next-gen version that offers everything  the new enemy placements the new enemies  the different law everything and that's  what I got to play this week I made the  trip down to San Francisco where I got  to play the full game and I got to learn  about what exactly was new so I hope I  can help you guys decide whether you'd  want to play another playthrough or  whether you'd like to make another  purchase so there are going to be three  major things that might entice you to  play there's improved enemy placements  improved graphics and better  storytelling so let's start with enemy  placements and what I got to see the  first thing everybody at the event  noticed was this ogre he wasn't there  before  you have to sneak past him and it  introduces a stealth element centered  around an enemy new players definitely  should have realized is difficult at  this point further up in a clearing up  the ladder there's almost three times as  many hollows lying in wait for you and  getting through this section is like  going through a minefield trying to only  aggro one enemy at a time and trying not  to get overwhelmed in the next section  there's now an archer shooting at you  while you fight other enemies in melee  so these areas and the enemies within  them are designed way better than they  were previously and as you play through  the entire game you realize that most  sections that were lacking before have  improvements like these now  you'll see a bunch of them in the  footage being shown I'm sure some are  better because the enemies are in  different places and some flat-out have  different enemies that just make more  sense in those locations expect some  hate nights actually in hade and more  desert Sorceress's in harvest valley you  know that kind of thing since this game  is running on better hardware the AI is  better two enemies will chase you  practically forever now from what I  could tell  so they'll catch you if you try to run  through an area and you also notice  enemies patrolling now you know moving  around in the environment which forces  you to learn their patterns and  sometimes can make an area feel a bit  more real and a bit more lived-in if  that makes sense so the elements of the  better AI like having enemies follow you  further was explained to me as a result  of having better hardware and speaking  of better hardware let's talk about the  graphics and the lighting in this build  so you should have got a pretty good  feel of what it looks like simply put  the game feels like you're playing on PC  it's 60 FPS it's got sharper textures  and that's all great but it's not quite  as pretty as the demo was which I know a  lot of you were hoping for me included  but it is better than the console and  the PC versions from what I can tell and  I am really happy to announce that one  feature of the demo has made it through  to the next-gen versions of the game the  dark areas you first notice this in the  basement that you can unlock after the  last giant so everything immediately  becomes pitch black just like it did in  the demo and you finally get to make  that decision should I use a torch in my  off hand and see more or should I use a  shield and defend against the things  coming at me in the darkness so I'm sure  it's no coincidence that all of the  enemies in the dark area have changed so  you won't know what's in there and you  won't know what's coming at you but the  thing is the environments haven't  changed so for an experienced player who  knows how to navigate these areas you  probably won't spend very long in the  dark areas because you'll just dash  right through and that's a bit of a -  but for new players this is awesome and  I'm really  be for them to be honest it wasn't dark  in as many areas as I hoped it would be  but at least they've upped the contrast  so you can't just go through dark areas  with your brightness settings turned up  anymore and it's cool to have this  implemented but like I said better for  new players not as great for experienced  players so the world hasn't changed but  there are more significant encounters  and I imagine for a new player the  entire game is going to be a much more  memorable experience but for an old  player an experienced player coming back  the most they're probably going to get  out of you is a that's new kind of  feeling and that's what I got when I was  going through the game isn't a  completely revolutionised experience so  you shouldn't buy it or play it  expecting that one of the biggest things  that will change your experience though  is the placement of weapons I found the  grand Lance shortly after the last giant  and I used it all the way until the  demon of song so that was a refreshing  character change for me usually I pick  up a weapon like the S stock or the  great sword and I pretty much go through  most of the game with that but since all  the weapons are switched around it means  that you'll be crafting entirely  different characters to tackle a  slightly different experience so that's  pretty cool but overall if you liked  Dark Souls 2 then you'll like it even  more but if you don't like Dark Souls 2  because the level design is lacking or  because you didn't like the enemies or  the bosses then nothing scholar of the  first sin will do can fix that the world  is the same the flow of the game is the  same the enemy designs are the same and  for you the only thing that might be  interesting is the story item  descriptions have changed in a big way  pretty much everyone I read had an extra  paragraph added to it which you'll be  able to see when the patch drops On  February 5th so you can expect a lot of  videos on this stuff and even if you  don't play you'll be able to see that  and experience that so make sure you're  subscribed for that but mostly though I  expect the major story significance to  come towards the end of the game  so overall everyone's going to be in a  different camp with scholar of the first  sin  and there are a lot of options available  to you I'd really love if you could tell  me what you plan on doing are you going  to download the patch and just meet the  scholar of the first sin or will you  wait until April and play through with  updated graphics and updated enemy  behaviors and enemy placements or will  you do neither of those things and are  you interested in watching my videos on  the scholar of the first sin so there's  a straw poll down below and a comment  section for you all to vote in so I can  try to figure out how many of you are  actually interested in this and it's  also worth noting that I'm really  confident that PC players will be  getting a discount when repurchasing the  game but you really should definitely  wait for an official statement on that  so thanks for watching guys I'll be  bringing you some scholar of the first  sin and also some blood-borne stuff as  soon as I get back home so there's a lot  of stuff to cover in the next few months  and I'll talk to you next time Cheers
 

